  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $-{2}^{2x-3}{x}^{2}+3x\\cdot {2}^{2x-3}>-4\\cdot {2}^{2x-3}.$\n\n{{field-1}} $<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{2}^{2x-3}{x}^{2}+3x\\cdot {2}^{2x-3} & >-4\\cdot {2}^{2x-3} \\\\ (-{x}^{2}+3x+4){2}^{2x-3} & >0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-3x-4){2}^{2x-3} & >0 \\\\ -(x+1)(x-4){2}^{2x-3} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${2}^{2x-3}$ and get\n\n$$\n-(x+1)(x-4)>0.\n$$\n\nThis represents a downwards parabola whose roots are at $x=-1$ and $x=4.$ The parabola is above the $x$-axis when $-1<x<4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013930/assets/df6c1c0ac26d9074f8c726b2265780fefad631561d7a729d2dfc7998ef043859.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $-1<x<4.$",
